<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Srebrenica</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Srebrenica is a town in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia-and-herzegovina">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a> near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drina">Drina</a>, whose name comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> mined there. From the 14th century its mines, worked by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> miners and controlled by Ragusan merchants, made it one of the richest places in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>, contested by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> until the Ottoman conquest. In July 1995 Bosnian Serb forces killed more than 8,000 Bosniak men and boys there, an act ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a> by international courts.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/srebrenica.jpg" alt="Srebrenica" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jelle Visser · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Srebrenica_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Srebrenica is a small mountain town and municipality in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia-and-herzegovina">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a>, in the Republika Srpska entity. Its name means place or land of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, the same sense as its old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> name Argentaria. Illyrians mined silver nearby, and silver was a main reason for the Roman invasion of the area. A Roman settlement called Domavia stood near a mine, and ore was carried along the Via Argentaria to the mints at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amfissa">Salona</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sirmium">Sirmium</a>. Mineral springs, notably the Crni Guber, later supported a spa, which drew tourists in the Yugoslav period and brought the town an annual income of about three million dollars.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the 13th and 14th centuries the region belonged to the Banate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> and then the Bosnian Kingdom. The name first appears in 1376, when Srebrenica was already an important trading centre. Merchants of the Republic of Ragusa controlled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> trade and its export through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dubrovnik">Dubrovnik</a>, and many German miners settled there in the 14th century. Armed conflicts over the mines were frequent: according to the historian Konstantin Josef Jireček, between 1411 and 1463 the town changed hands several times among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, Bosnia and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>.</p><p>Under Ottoman rule its economic importance declined, and a Franciscan church was converted into the White Mosque. Austria-Hungary occupied the town in 1878, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-berlin">Congress of Berlin</a>, and introduced modern administration, schools and a postal service.</p><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnian-war">Bosnian War</a>, Serb forces occupied the town in April 1992, and Bosniak resistance groups retook it. Refugees from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drina">Drina</a> valley gathered there, and it was besieged. On 16 April 1993 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> declared the enclave a safe area, guarded by a small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> unit with no permission to use force. In July 1995 the Army of Republika Srpska under General Ratko Mladić captured it.</p><p>Men of fighting age and teenage boys who fell into Bosnian Serb hands were killed in a systematically organised series of summary executions, 8,372 men and boys in all, while women, boys under 12 and men over 65 were taken by bus to Tuzla.</p><p>The International Criminal Tribunal for the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> ruled in 2001 that this was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a>, confirmed on appeal in 2004, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-court-of-justice">International Court of Justice</a> upheld the finding in 2007.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Under the 1995 Dayton Agreement Srebrenica was placed in the Republika Srpska. Its population is roughly half Muslim and half <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a>, and most of the 23 mosques destroyed in the war have been rebuilt. The White Mosque dates to the 17th century, and the Sase Monastery is a Serbian Orthodox monastery dating back to the 13th century. The Church of the Intercession of the Holy Virgin dates to 1903.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Srebrenica” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It joined NATO as a founding member, sent a battalion to Korea, and in 1995 its peacekeepers failed to prevent the massacre at <b>Srebrenica</b>, a failure that led to the government’s resignation in 2002.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Kingdom of the Netherlands · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Its merchants carried the silver and lead of the Serbian and Bosnian mines, at Novo Brdo, <b>Srebrenica</b> and elsewhere, together with wax, hides, wool and slaves, from the Balkan interior to the coast, and returned with cloth, salt, wine and luxury goods.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-ragusa">Republic of Ragusa · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Srebrenica</b>, Rudnik, Trepča, Novo Brdo, Kopaonik, Majdanpek, Brskovo and Samokov were centres for iron, copper, lead, silver and gold.</blockquote><a href="/empire/serbian-empire">Serbian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The war had killed about 100,000 people, including some 8,000 at <b>Srebrenica</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-21">21 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Stephen II Kotromanić (1322–1353) Bosnia annexed Zachlumia and the land up to the Cetina, and Saxon miners opened silver and lead mines at <b>Srebrenica</b> and Fojnica, trading through Ragusa.</blockquote><a href="/state/banate-of-bosnia">Banate of Bosnia</a></li><li><blockquote>Silver from <b>Srebrenica</b> supported the state, and the Bosnian Church, Catholicism and Orthodoxy coexisted.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-bosnia">Kingdom of Bosnia</a></li></ul></section>
